    "deloadMethodSummary": "In a mesocycle's deload week, each exercise keeps about half its working sets (half, rounded up, so 4 becomes 2 and 3 becomes 2) at 90% of the planned weight, rounded down to your equipment, and a step lighter where 90% would round back to the planned weight. Reps stay as planned and warm-ups stay. The week after a deload carries on from the week before it. You keep training as often as usual. Lifters and coaches deload mostly by cutting volume and effort while keeping frequency. In a survey of strength and physique athletes, a typical deload lasted about a week every 5 to 6 weeks.",
    "@deloadMethodSummary": {},
    "deloadMethodLimitations": "Deloading is widely practised but little studied. The one trial found that a full week off mid-programme did not change muscle growth and slightly reduced strength gains, so the benefit is mainly recovery and practicality.",
    "@deloadMethodLimitations": {},
    "deloadMethodOwnChoices": "Half the sets, 90% of the weight (the middle of 85–95%), and keeping warm-ups are Compound's own choices. When the deload falls is set by the mesocycle.",
